dge built?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "The Frommer's travel guide describes the Golden Gate Bridge as ``possibly the most beautiful, certainly the most photographed, bridge in the world. ''At the time of its opening in 1937, it was both the longest and the tallest suspension bridge in the world, with a main span of 4,200 feet (1,280 m) and a total height of 746 feet (227 m).", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that the Golden Gate Bridge was opened in 1937. The question asks for the year it was built, and since the opening year is typically the year a structure is considered built, the answer is 1937.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', '1937', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The city where Blue Cheer was founded is San Francisco. The Golden Gate Bridge was built in 1937. Therefore, the answer is 1937.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', '1937', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 934, 'user': 'What follows the character set into which ASCII was incorporated?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks what follows the character set into which ASCII was incorporated. To answer this, I first need to identify another character set that incorporated ASCII, as this will help determine what came after ASCII in the sequence of character set evolution.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'ASCII was incorporated into what other character set?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'ASCII was incorporated into the Unicode character set as the first 128 symbols, so the 7-bit ASCII characters have the same numeric codes in both sets. This allows UTF-8 to be backward compatible with 7-bit ASCII, as a UTF-8 file containing only ASCII characters is identical to an ASCII file containing the same sequence of characters. Even more importantly, forward compatibility is ensured as software that recognizes only 7-bit ASCII characters as special and does not alter bytes with the highest bit set (as is often done to support 8-bit ASCII extensions such as ISO-8859-1) will preserve UTF-8 data unchanged.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', '.ASCII was incorporated into the Unicode character set, as stated in the context. The text explicitly mentions that ASCII was incorporated into Unicode as the first 128 symbols, which directly leads to the answer "Unicode.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Unicode', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', "The original question asks about what comes after ASCII in the character set hierarchy. To address this, I first need to understand the broader context of ASCII's place in the character set hierarchy, which leads to the sub-question about what follows ASCII. This sub-question sets the foundation for identifying the next level in the character encoding structure.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'What follows ASCII in the character set hierarchy?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The Latin-1 Supplement (also called C1 Controls and Latin-1 Supplement) is the second Unicode block in the Unicode standard. It encodes the upper range of ISO 8859-1: 80 (U+0080) - FF (U+00FF). Controls C1 (0080–009F) are not graphic. This block ranges from U+0080 to U+00FF, contains 128 characters and includes the C1 controls, Latin-1 punctuation and symbols, 30 pairs of majuscule and minuscule accented Latin characters and 2 mathematical operators.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'Answer: ISO 8859-1\n\nTo form the answer, we first identify the question: "What follows ASCII in the character set hierarchy?" The context explains that the Latin-1 Supplement is the second Unicode block, which encodes the upper range of ISO 8859-1 (from U+0080 to U+00FF). Since ASCII covers the range from U+0000 to U+007F, the next set of characters in the hierarchy is ISO 8859-1, which follows ASCII. Therefore, the answer is ISO 8859-1.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'ISO 8859-1', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks what follows the character set into which ASCII was incorporated. First, we determine that ASCII was incorporated into Unicode. Then, we identify what follows ASCII in the character set hierarchy, which is ISO 8859-1. Therefore, the final answer is ISO 8859-1.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'ISO 8859-1', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 789, 'user': 'When did the person who said "Child is the father of man" live?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the lifespan of the person who said "Child is the father of man." To determine this, I first need to identify who made this famous quote, as that will lead me to the individual\'s historical context and time period.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who said 'Child is the father of man'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "``Child is father of the man'' is an idiom originating from the poem ``My Heart Leaps Up'' by William Wordsworth. In a 1966 interview, Wilson mistakenly attributed it to Karl Menninger, and added that the saying had fascinated him. There exist many different interpretations of the phrase, the most popular of which is man being the product of habits and behavior developed in youth. According to collaborator Van Dyke Parks, he brought up the idiom to Wilson.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The answer is formed by identifying the origin of the phrase "Child is the father of man" from the context. The context states that the phrase originates from William Wordsworth\'s poem "My Heart Leaps Up," which directly attributes the saying to him. Therefore, the answer is William Wordsworth.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'William Wordsworth', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the lifespan of the person who said "Child is the father of man." To answer this, I first need to identify who made that quote. Since it is known that William Wordsworth wrote those words, the next step is to determine when he lived.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When did William Wordsworth live?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "The French cleric Pierre Gassendi (1592-1665) represented the materialist tradition in opposition to the attempts of René Descartes (1596-1650) to provide the natural sciences with dualist foundations. There followed the materialist and atheist abbé Jean Meslier (1664-1729), Julien Offray de La Mettrie, the German-French Paul-Henri Thiry Baron d'Holbach (1723-1789), the Encyclopedist Denis Diderot (1713-1784), and other French Enlightenment thinkers; as well as (in England) John ``Walking'' Stewart (1747-1822), whose insistence in seeing matter as endowed with a moral dimension had a major impact on the philosophical poetry of William Wordsworth (1770-1850).", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'William Wordsworth is mentioned in the context as a philosophical poet influenced by John ``Walking\'\' Stewart. The text provides his lifespan as "1770-1850," which directly answers the question about when he lived. Therefore, the answer is formed by identifying and extracting the date range from the context.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', '1770-1850', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine when the person who said "Child is the father of man" lived, first identify the speaker of the quote, which is William Wordsworth. Then, find the time period during which William Wordsworth lived, which is 1770-1850. Therefore, the final answer is 1770-1850.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', '1770-1850', '</answer>\n']}
