{'id': 4815, 'user': 'For whom did the performer of Awaiting on You All write the song Something?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks for the person for whom the performer of "Awaiting on You All" wrote the song "Something." To answer this, I first need to identify who the performer of "Awaiting on You All" is, as this is the key to determining the intended recipient of the song.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who is the performer of 'Awaiting on You All'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', '"Awaiting on You All" is a song by English musician George Harrison, released on his 1970 triple album, "All Things Must Pass". Along with the single "My Sweet Lord", it is among the more overtly religious compositions on "All Things Must Pass", and the recording typifies co-producer Phil Spector\'s influence on the album, due to his liberal use of reverberation and other Wall of Sound production techniques. Harrison recorded the track in London backed by musicians such as Eric Clapton, Bobby Whitlock, Klaus Voormann, Jim Gordon and Jim Price – many of whom he had toured with, as Delaney & Bonnie and Friends, in December 1969, while still officially a member of the Beatles. Musically, the composition reflects Harrison\'s embracing of the gospel music genre, following his production of fellow Apple Records artists Billy Preston and Doris Troy.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The answer is formed by identifying the performer mentioned in the context. The context explicitly states that "Awaiting on You All" is a song by George Harrison. Therefore, the answer is George Harrison.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'George Harrison', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks for the person for whom the performer of "Awaiting on You All" wrote the song "Something." Since "Awaiting on You All" is a song by George Harrison, the next logical step is to determine for whom he wrote "Something," which directly answers the original question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "For whom did George Harrison write the song 'Something'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "The opening lyric was taken from the title of ``Something in the Way She Moves '', a track by Harrison's fellow Apple Records artist James Taylor. While Harrison imagined the composition in the style of Ray Charles, his inspiration for`` Something'' was his wife, Pattie Boyd. In her 2007 autobiography, Wonderful Today, Boyd recalls: ``He told me, in a matter-of-fact way, that he had written it for me. I thought it was beautiful... ''Boyd discusses the song's popularity among other recording artists and concludes:`` My favourite (version) was the one by George Harrison, which he played to me in the kitchen at Kinfauns.''", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The answer is derived from the context, which states that George Harrison\'s inspiration for the song "Something" was his wife, Pattie Boyd. The text explicitly mentions that he wrote the song for her, as she recalls him telling her, "He told me, in a matter-of-fact way, that he had written it for me." This directly leads to the conclusion that the song was written for his wife, Pattie Boyd.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'his wife, Pattie Boyd', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The performer of "Awaiting on You All" is George Harrison. George Harrison wrote the song "Something" for his wife, Pattie Boyd. Therefore, the final answer is his wife, Pattie Boyd.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'his wife, Pattie Boyd', '</answer>\n']}
