futed the claim that the Queen was critical of the European Union, which was Deputy Prime Minist
{'id': 11910, 'user': 'when was the city where Bernardo de Balbuena died annexed by the United States?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'To answer the original question about when the city where Bernardo de Balbuena died was annexed by the United States, I first need to identify the specific city where he died. Without knowing the city, it is impossible to determine the annexation date.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'In what city did Bernardo de Balbuena die?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Bernardo de Balbuena (c. Valdepeñas (Spain) 1561 – San Juan, Puerto Rico, October 1627) was a Spanish poet. He was the first of a long series of Latin American poets who extolled the special beauties of the New World.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the answer, we examine the context provided. The context states that Bernardo de Balbuena was born in Valdepeñas, Spain, and died in San Juan, Puerto Rico, on October 1627. The question asks for the city of his death, so the answer is directly given as San Juan, Puerto Rico.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'San Juan, Puerto Rico', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To answer the original question about when the city where Bernardo de Balbuena died was annexed by the United States, I first need to identify the city in question. Since Bernardo de Balbuena died in San Juan, I must determine when San Juan, Puerto Rico was annexed by the United States. This sub-question is the first step in locating the relevant historical event.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When was San Juan, Puerto Rico annexed by the United States?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "In 1898, during the Spanish-American War, Puerto Rico was invaded and subsequently became a possession of the United States. The first years of the 20th century were marked by the struggle to obtain greater democratic rights from the United States. The Foraker Act of 1900, which established a civil government, and the Jones Act of 1917, which made Puerto Ricans U.S. citizens,