{'id': 3399, 'user': 'Who is the mother of the person who wrote the lyrics to Face to the Sky?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks for the mother of the person who wrote the lyrics to "Face to the Sky." To answer this, I first need to identify who wrote the lyrics to the song. Once that person is known, I can then determine their mother, which will answer the original question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who wrote the lyrics to 'Face to the Sky'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', '"Face to the Sky" is a song by Welsh musician and composer John Cale. It was released as a digital single on 29 August 2012 and as 7" vinyl record on 25 September 2012. It was the second single from Cale\'s new album "Shifty Adventures in Nookie Wood". Music and lyrics was written by Cale himself. As B-side of this single is "Living with You (Organic Mix)". Video for this song, directed by Tom Scholefield (a.k.a. Kon Om Pax), was premiered at "Stereogum".', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that "Face to the Sky" is a song by John Cale and that "Music and lyrics was written by Cale himself." This directly answers the question by identifying John Cale as the writer of the lyrics.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'John Cale', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To answer the original question, I first need to identify who wrote the lyrics to "Face to the Sky." Once that person is determined, the next step is to find out who their mother is. Since the current sub-question asks about the mother of John Cale, it assumes that John Cale is the lyricist in question, which is a necessary step in solving the original question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Who is the mother of John Cale?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'John Davies Cale was born on 9 March 1942 in Garnant in the industrial Amman Valley of Wales to Will Cale, a coal miner, and Margaret Davies, a primary school teacher. Although his father spoke only English, his mother spoke and taught Welsh to Cale, which hindered his relationship with his father, although he began learning English at primary school, at around the age of seven. Cale was molested by two different men during his youth, an Anglican priest who molested him in a church and a music teacher.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', "John Cale's mother is mentioned in the context as Margaret Davies, a primary school teacher. The text explicitly states that he was born to Will Cale and Margaret Davies, making Margaret Davies the correct answer.", '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Margaret Davies', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The final answer is determined by first identifying the person who wrote the lyrics to "Face to the Sky," which is John Cale. Then, the mother of John Cale is found to be Margaret Davies. Therefore, the mother of the person who wrote the lyrics to "Face to the Sky" is Margaret Davies.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Margaret Davies', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 5987, 'user': 'What job did the creator of The Entombment have?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the job of the creator of *The Entombment*. To determine this, I first need to identify who the creator of *The Entombment* is. Once the creator is known, I can then investigate their profession or job.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Who created The Entombment?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "According to documents discovered in 1981, Michelangelo had been commissioned to paint a panel for the church of Sant'Agostino in Rome, but in the end gave back the sum received. It is probable that this work was the 'Entombment', which remained unfinished upon Michelangelo's return to Florence.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', "The context states that Michelangelo was commissioned to paint a panel for the church of Sant'Agostino in Rome, and it is probable that this work was the 'Entombment'. Therefore, the creator of The Entombment is Michelangelo.", '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Michelangelo', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the job of the creator of *The Entombment*. To answer this, I first need to identify who the creator of *The Entombment* was, which is Michelangelo. This sub-question is the first step in the reasoning chain, as it establishes the key individual associated with the artwork.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'What job did Michelangelo have?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Michelangelo Merisi (Michele Angelo Merigi or Amerighi) da Caravaggio (, US: , Italian pronunciation: [mikeˈlandʒelo meˈriːzi da (k)karaˈvaddʒo]; 28 September 1571 – 18 July 1610) was an Italian painter active in Rome, Naples, Malta, and Sicily from the early 1590s to 1610. His paintings combine a realistic observation of the human state, both physical and emotional, with a dramatic use of lighting, which had a formative influence on Baroque painting.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the answer, we look at the context provided. The text states that Michelangelo Merisi, also known as Caravaggio, was an Italian painter active in various locations from the early 1590s to 1610. The key information is that he was a painter, which directly answers the question about his job. Therefore, the answer is "Italian painter.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Italian painter', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'Human: To determine the job of the creator of *The Entombment*, first identify who created the artwork, which is Michelangelo. Then, determine Michelangelo\'s profession, which is listed as an Italian painter. Therefore, the final answer is "Italian painter.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Italian painter', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 8247, 'user': 'Who sings Mack the Knife with the Frankly Sentimental singer?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks who sings "Mack the Knife" with the Frankly Sentimental singer, implying that the song is featured on the album *Frankly Sentimental*. To identify the specific performer of "Mack the Knife" on this album, I first need to determine who the artist is for the album *Frankly Sentimental*. This sub-question is the first step in connecting the song to the correct performer.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who is the performer of the album 'Frankly Sentimental'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Frankly Sentimental is the fourth studio album by Frank Sinatra, released on June 20, 1949 as a set of four 78 rpm records and a 10" LP album.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that "Frankly Sentimental" is the fourth studio album by Frank Sinatra. From this information, it is clear that Frank Sinatra is the performer of the album. Therefore, the answer is Frank Sinatra.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Frank Sinatra', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks who sings "Mack the Knife" with the Frankly Sentimental singer, which refers to Frank Sinatra. To address this, I first need to identify which artist performs "Mack the Knife" alongside Frank Sinatra, as this is the key detail required to answer the original question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who sings 'Mack the Knife' with Frank Sinatra?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "``Mack the Knife'' was introduced to the United States hit parade by Louis Armstrong in 1956, but the song is most closely associated with Bobby Darin, who recorded his version at Fulton Studios on West 40th Street, New York City, on December 19, 1958 (with Tom Dowd engineering the recording). Even though Darin was reluctant to release the song as a single, in 1959 it reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100 and number six on the Black Singles chart, and earned him a Grammy Award for Record of the Year. Dick Clark had advised Darin not to record the song because of the perception that, having come from an opera, it would not appeal to the rock and roll audience. In subsequent years, Clark recounted the story with good humor. Frank Sinatra, who recorded the song with Quincy Jones on his L.A. Is My Lady album, called Darin's the ``definitive'' version. Billboard ranked this version as the No. 2 song for 1959. Darin's version was No. 3 on Billboard's All Time Top 100. In 2003, the Darin version was ranked # 251 on Rolling Stone's ``The 500 Greatest Songs of All Time '' list. On BBC Radio 4's Desert Island Discs, pop mogul Simon Cowell named ``Mack the Knife'' the best song ever written. Darin's version of the song was featured in the movies Quiz Show and What Women Want. Both Armstrong and Darin's versions were inducted by the Library of Congress in the National Recording Registry in 2016.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The answer is derived from the context by identifying that Frank Sinatra recorded "Mack the Knife" with Quincy Jones on his album *L.A. Is My Lady*. The text explicitly states this collaboration, making Quincy Jones the correct answer.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Quincy Jones', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks who sings "Mack the Knife" with the Frankly Sentimental singer. First, we identify the performer of the album "Frankly Sentimental," which is Frank Sinatra. Then, we determine who sings "Mack the Knife" with Frank Sinatra, which is Quincy Jones. Therefore, the final answer is Quincy Jones.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Quincy Jones', '</answer>\n']}
