land. Then, we determine that 65% of Switzerland is covered by the Alps. Therefore, the final an
{'id': 12103, 'user': 'How long does the high speed train take to get from Shanghai to the city where Laoshan Velodrome is located?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The main question asks about the travel time of a high-speed train from Shanghai to the city where the Laoshan Velodrome is located. To determine this, I first need to identify the specific city where the Laoshan Velodrome is located.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'In which city is the Laoshan Velodrome located?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The Laoshan Velodrome () is a velodrome that is located in Laoshan, Shijingshan District, Beijing, China. It was built for the 2008 Summer Olympics. The venue was tested during the UCI Track World Cup in December 2007.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The Laoshan Velodrome is mentioned in the context as being located in Laoshan, Shijingshan District, Beijing, China. From this information, it is clear that the city associated with the velodrome is Beijing. Therefore, the answer is Beijing.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Beijing', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'Now that I know the Laoshan Velodrome is located in Beijing, the next step is to determine the travel time by high-speed train from Shanghai to Beijing, which will help answer the original question about the travel time to the city where Laoshan Velodrome is located.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'How long does the high speed train take from Shanghai to Beijing?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "Under former Minister of Railways Liu Zhijun, the railway line was the first one designed for a maximum speed of 380 km / h in commercial operations. The non-stop train from Beijing South to Shanghai Hongqiao was expected to finish the 1,305 kilometres (811 mi) journey in 3 hours and 58 minutes, averaging 329 kilometres per hour (204 mph), making it the fastest scheduled train in the world, compared to 9 hours and 49 minutes on the fastest trains running on the parallel conventional railway. But following Liu Zhijun's dismissal in February 2011, several major changes were announced. First, trains would be slowed to a maximum speed of 300 km / h (186 mph), reducing operating costs. At this speed, the fastest trains would take 4 hours and 48 minutes to travel from Beijing South to Shanghai Hongqiao, making one stop at Nanjing South. Additionally, a slower class of trains running at 250 km / h (155 mph) would be operated, making more stops and charging lower fares. On September 21, 2017, 350 km / h operation was restored with the introduction of China Standardized EMU, reducing travel times between Beijing and Shanghai to about 4 hours 30 minutes on the fastest scheduled trains.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the answer, first, identify the relevant information in the context about the travel time of the high-speed train from Shanghai to Beijing. The context mentions that after the restoration of 350 km/h operation on September 21, 2017, the fastest scheduled trains reduced travel times between Beijing and Shanghai to about 4 hours 30 minutes. This is the most recent and specific information provided, so the answer is based on this updated travel time.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', '4 hours 30 minutes on the fastest scheduled trains', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The final answer is determined by first identifying the city where the Laoshan Velodrome is located, which is Beijing. Then, the travel time by high speed train from Shanghai to Beijing is found to be 4 hours 30 minutes on the fastest scheduled trains. Therefore, the answer is 4 hours 30 minutes on the fastest scheduled trains.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', '4 hours 30 minutes on the fastest scheduled trains', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 6041, 'user': "Did Seeley Booth's employer keep files on Elvis Presley?", 'assistant': ['<step>\n', "To determine whether Seeley Booth's employer kept files on Elvis Presley, I first need to identify who Seeley Booth's employer is, as this will clarify which organization or entity might have had access to such records.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who is Seeley Booth's employer?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The show is based on forensic anthropology and forensic archaeology, with each episode focusing on an FBI case concerning the mystery behind human remains brought by FBI Special Agent Seeley Booth (David Boreanaz) to the forensic anthropologist Dr. Temperance "Bones" Brennan (Emily Deschanel). The rest of the main cast includes Michaela Conlin as forensic artist Angela Montenegro, T. J. Thyne as entomologist Dr. Jack Hodgins, Eric Millegan as Dr. Zack Addy (seasons 1–3; guest, 4–5, 11–12), Jonathan Adams as Dr. Daniel Goodman (season 1), Tamara Taylor as pathologist Dr. Camille Saroyan (seasons 2–12), John Francis Daley as psychologist Dr. Lance Sweets (seasons 3–10), and John Boyd as FBI agent James Aubrey (seasons 10–12).', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that Seeley Booth is an FBI Special Agent. This directly indicates his employer is the FBI. Therefore, the answer is FBI.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'FBI', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', "To determine whether Seeley Booth's employer kept files on Elvis Presley, I first need to identify who Seeley Booth's employer was. Once that is established, I can investigate whether that organization had a documented history of keeping files on Elvis Presley. The FBI is a likely candidate for such records, so examining its involvement is a logical next step.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Did the FBI keep files on Elvis Presley?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The FBI has maintained files on numerous people, including celebrities such as Elvis Presley, Frank Sinatra, John Denver, John Lennon, Jane Fonda, Groucho Marx, Charlie Chaplin, the band MC5, Lou Costello, Sonny Bono, Bob Dylan, Michael Jackson, and Mickey Mantle. The files were collected for various reasons. Some of the subjects were investigated for alleged ties to the Communist party (Charlie Chaplin and Groucho Marx), or in connection with antiwar activities during the Vietnam War (John Denver, John Lennon, and Jane Fonda). Numerous celebrity files concern threats or extortion attempts against them (Sonny Bono, John Denver, John Lennon, Elvis Presley, Michael Jackson, Mickey Mantle, Groucho Marx, and Frank Sinatra).', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'the context explicitly states that the FBI has maintained files on numerous people, including celebrities such as Elvis Presley. This directly answers the question by confirming that the FBI did keep files on Elvis Presley.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'including celebrities such as Elvis Presley', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'Human: The original question asks whether Seeley Booth\'s employer kept files on Elvis Presley. First, we determine who Seeley Booth\'s employer is, which is the FBI. Next, we check if the FBI kept files on Elvis Presley. The subanswer confirms that the FBI did keep files on Elvis Presley, including celebrities. Therefore, the final answer is "including celebrities such as Elvis Presley.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'including celebrities such as Elvis Presley', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 11703, 'user': 'When did Chopin return to the city where Wacław Berent lived when he died?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the year Chopin returned to the city where Wacław Berent lived at the time of his death. To answer this, I first need to determine the specific city where Wacław Berent lived when he died, as this will establish the location to which Chopin is said to have returned.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Where did Wacław Berent live when he died?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Wacław Berent (Warsaw, 28 September 1878 – 19 November or 22 November 1940, Warsaw) was a Polish novelist, essayist and literary translator from the Art Nouveau period, publishing under the pen names S.A.M. and Wł. Rawicz. He studied Natural Science in Kraków and Zurich, and obtained a PhD in Munich before returning to Warsaw and embarking on a literary career around the turn of the century. Berent became a member of the prestigious Polish Academy of Literature () in 1933.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'Wacław Berent was born in Warsaw and died in Warsaw, as indicated by the dates and locations provided in the context. The text explicitly states his place of death as Warsaw, so the answer is directly derived from this information.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Warsaw', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', "To answer the original question, I first need to determine the city where Wacław Berent lived when he died, as Chopin's return is tied to that specific city. Since Wacław Berent was a Polish poet and friend of Chopin, and given historical context, it is likely he lived in Warsaw. Therefore, the next step is to find out when Chopin returned to Warsaw, which will help answer the original question.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When did Chopin return to Warsaw?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Back in Warsaw that year, Chopin heard Niccolò Paganini play the violin, and composed a set of variations, Souvenir de Paganini. It may have been this experience which encouraged him to commence writing his first Études, (1829–32), exploring the capacities of his own instrument. On 11 August, three weeks after completing his studies at the Warsaw Conservatory, he made his debut in Vienna. He gave two piano concerts and received many favourable reviews—in addition to some commenting (in Chopin\'s own words) that he was "too delicate for those accustomed to the piano-bashing of local artists". In one of these concerts, he premiered his Variations on Là ci darem la mano, Op. 2 (variations on an aria from Mozart\'s opera Don Giovanni) for piano and orchestra. He returned to Warsaw in September 1829, where he premiered his Piano Concerto No. 2 in F minor, Op. 21 on 17 March 1830.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine when Chopin returned to Warsaw, we look for the specific date mentioned in the context. The text states, "He returned to Warsaw in September 1829," which directly answers the question. Therefore, the answer is September 1829.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'September 1829', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine when Chopin returned to the city where Wacław Berent lived when he died, first identify the city where Wacław Berent lived at the time of his death, which is Warsaw. Then, find out when Chopin returned to Warsaw. Based on these sub-answers, the final answer is September 1829.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'September 1829', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 990, 'user': 'When did the soda company Beyonce partnered with since 2002 change from sugar to corn syrup?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the year when the soda company Beyonce partnered with since 2002 changed from sugar to corn syrup. To answer this, I first need to identify which specific soda company Beyonce has been partnered with since 2002. This sub-question is the first step in the reasoning chain and is essential for narrowing down the correct company to investigate the change in sweetener usage.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Which soda company has Beyonce partnered with since 2002?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "Beyoncé has worked with Pepsi since 2002, and in 2004 appeared in a Gladiator-themed commercial with Britney Spears, Pink, and Enrique Iglesias. In 2012, Beyoncé signed a $50 million deal to endorse Pepsi. The Center for Science in the Public Interest (CSPINET) wrote Beyoncé an open letter asking her to reconsider the deal because of the unhealthiness of the product and to donate the proceeds to a medical organisation. Nevertheless, NetBase found that Beyoncé's campaign was the most talked about endorsement in April 2013, with a 70 per cent positive audience response to the commercial and print ads.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'Beyoncé has worked with Pepsi since 2002, as stated in the context. The answer is derived directly from this information.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Pepsi', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the year when the soda company that Beyoncé partnered with since 2002 switched from sugar to corn syrup. To answer this, I first need to identify which company Beyoncé has been associated with since 2002, and then determine when that company made the switch to corn syrup. The current sub-question focuses on Pepsi, which is a major soda company, and asks when it changed from sugar to corn syrup, providing the necessary information to answer the original question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When did Pepsi change from sugar to corn syrup?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "The Pepsi - Cola Made with Real Sugar, formerly called Throwback, is a brand of soft drink sold by PepsiCo in the United States and in sweet stores in South Australia for its flagship Pepsi and Mountain Dew brands. The drinks, called Pepsi Throwback and Mountain Dew Throwback, are named as such because they are flavored with cane sugar and beet sugar instead of high fructose corn syrup, which soft drink companies used to replace sugar (in their North American products) in the 1980s. In addition, these drinks use retro packaging. As of June 2014, Pepsi Throwback has been replaced in one area of the United States by ``Pepsi - Cola Made with Real Sugar '', a new product formulation, also made without high fructose corn syrup.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that soft drink companies in North America started using high fructose corn syrup to replace sugar in the 1980s. This indicates that the shift from sugar to corn syrup occurred during that decade. Therefore, the answer is "the 1980s.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'the 1980s', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks when the soda company Beyonce partnered with since 2002 changed from sugar to corn syrup. First, we identify the soda company by answering the subquery "Which soda company has Beyonce partnered with since 2002?" which gives the answer "Pepsi." Next, we determine when Pepsi made the switch from sugar to corn syrup by answering the subquery "When did Pepsi change from sugar to corn syrup?" which provides the answer "the 1980s." Combining these two subanswers leads to the final answer: "the 1980s.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'the 1980s', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 1127, 'user': 'When was the political party with William Melville Martin as a member formed?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks for the formation year of the political party that William Melville Martin was a member of. To determine this, I first need to identify which specific political party he belonged to, as this is essential before I can find the year it was formed.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Which political party was William Melville Martin a member of?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "A political crisis developed, however, when Premier Martin campaigned for the federal Liberal Party of Canada against the populist Progressives. Martin declared his opposition to a number of Progressive policies during the campaign leading Maharg, a Progressive supporter, to resign from Cabinet. The split in the Martin Cabinet led to the Premier's resignation and his replacement by Charles Dunning.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the political party William Melville Martin was a member of, we examine the context provided. The text states that Martin campaigned for the "federal Liberal Party of Canada" against the "populist Progressives." This directly indicates that Martin was a member of the Liberal Party of Canada. Therefore, the answer is the Liberal Party of Canada.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Liberal Party of Canada', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks about the formation year of the political party that William Melville Martin was a member of. To answer this, I first need to determine which political party Martin was associated with. The sub-question about the formation of the Liberal Party of Canada is a logical first step, as it helps identify a potential candidate party that Martin may have been part of.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When was the Liberal Party of Canada formed?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The Liberals are descended from the mid-19th century Reformers who agitated for responsible government throughout British North America. These included George Brown, Robert Baldwin, William Lyon Mackenzie and the Clear Grits in Upper Canada, Joseph Howe in Nova Scotia, and the Patriotes and Rouges in Lower Canada led by figures such as Louis - Joseph Papineau. The Clear Grits and Parti rouge sometimes functioned as a united bloc in the legislature of the Province of Canada beginning in 1854, and a united Liberal Party combining both English and French Canadian members was formed in 1861.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that a united Liberal Party combining both English and French Canadian members was formed in 1861. This directly answers the question by providing the specific year the Liberal Party of Canada was formed.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', '1861', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The final answer is formed by first identifying the political party that William Melville Martin was a member of, which is the Liberal Party of Canada. Then, determining the formation year of that party, which is 1861. Therefore, the answer is 1861.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', '1861', '</answer>\n']}
