{'id': 6524, 'user': 'In what year did the employer of Edward O. Wiley start issuing degrees in engineering?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', "The original question asks about the year when the employer of Edward O. Wiley began issuing degrees in engineering. To determine this, I first need to identify who Edward O. Wiley's employer was, as this is the foundational step in answering the question. Without knowing the employer, it is impossible to proceed to the next step of finding when they started issuing engineering degrees.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'What is the employer of Edward O. Wiley?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Professor Edward Orlando Wiley III is the curator emeritus of ichthyology at the University of Kansas Biodiversity Institute and professor of systematics and evolution for the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at the University of Kansas. His Masters advisor was Darrell Hall, of Sam Houston State University (retired), and his doctoral advisor was Donn E. Rosen, of the American Museum of Natural History (deceased). Wiley has published extensively in topics related to phylogenetic systematics, is a Past President of the Society of Systematic Biology (then Zoology) and was involved in the founding of the Willi Hennig Society. Wiley is known for building on and establishing conceptual advances in the evolutionary species concept, first formulated by George Gaylord Simpson. Wiley defines an evolutionary species as:', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'University of Kansas is mentioned as the institution where Edward O. Wiley was a curator emeritus of ichthyology and a professor of systematics and evolution. These positions indicate that the University of Kansas is his employer.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'University of Kansas', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', "The original question asks about the year the employer of Edward O. Wiley began issuing degrees in engineering. To answer this, I first need to identify which institution was Edward O. Wiley's employer, and the most relevant lead is the University of Kansas, as it is commonly associated with him. The next step is to determine when the University of Kansas started issuing degrees in engineering.", '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'When did the University of Kansas start issuing degrees in engineering?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The KU School of Engineering is an ABET accredited, public engineering school located on the main campus. The School of Engineering was officially founded in 1891, although engineering degrees were awarded as early as 1873.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that engineering degrees were awarded as early as 1873, which indicates the earliest year the University of Kansas began issuing engineering degrees. Therefore, the answer is 1873.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', '1873', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', "The final answer is determined by first identifying Edward O. Wiley's employer, which is the University of Kansas. Then, we find out the year the University of Kansas began issuing degrees in engineering, which is 1873. Therefore, the answer is 1873.", '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', '1873', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 537, 'user': "what indie/folk act is based in Miami along with the singer of 'Cross Bones Style'?", 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks for an indie/folk act based in Miami that includes the singer of "Cross Bones Style." To identify this act, I first need to determine who the performer of "Cross Bones Style" is, as that will help narrow down the correct artist or band associated with both the song and the location.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', "Who is the performer of 'Cross Bones Style'?", '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', '"Cross Bones Style" is a song by the American singer/songwriter Cat Power, also known as Chan Marshall. It is the 10th song on her fourth album, "Moon Pix", released in September 1998.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', '# Thought Process\n\nTo answer the question "Who is the performer of \'Cross Bones Style\'?", we start by examining the context provided. The context states that "Cross Bones Style" is a song by the American singer/songwriter Cat Power, also known as Chan Marshall. This directly identifies the performer of the song. Since the question asks for the performer, the answer is simply the name given in the context, which is "Cat Power.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Cat Power', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The original question asks for an indie/folk act based in Miami that includes the singer of "Cross Bones Style." To answer this, I first need to identify the singer of "Cross Bones Style," which is Cat Power. Now that I know Cat Power is the key figure, the next step is to find another indie/folk musician who is also based in Miami, allowing me to identify the act that includes both.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Along with Cat Power, which indie/folk musician is based in Miami?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Miami is also considered a "hot spot" for dance music, Freestyle, a style of dance music popular in the 80\'s and 90\'s heavily influenced by Electro, hip-hop, and disco. Many popular Freestyle acts such as Pretty Tony, Debbie Deb, Stevie B, and Exposé, originated in Miami. Indie/folk acts Cat Power and Iron & Wine are based in the city, while alternative hip hop artist Sage Francis, electro artist Uffie, and the electroclash duo Avenue D were born in Miami, but musically based elsewhere. Also, ska punk band Against All Authority is from Miami, and rock/metal bands Nonpoint and Marilyn Manson each formed in neighboring Fort Lauderdale. Cuban American female recording artist, Ana Cristina, was born in Miami in 1985.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that both Cat Power and Iron & Wine are based in Miami. Since the question asks for the indie/folk musician based in Miami along with Cat Power, the answer is Iron & Wine.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Iron & Wine', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'rm the final answer, first identify the singer of "Cross Bones Style," which is Cat Power. Then, find an indie/folk act based in Miami, which is Iron & Wine. Since the question asks for the act based in Miami along with Cat Power, the answer is Iron & Wine.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Iron & Wine', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 9167, 'user': 'Who was the discoverer of the country that contains the islet of Faiava Lasi?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The main question asks about the discoverer of the country that contains the islet of Faiava Lasi. To identify the discoverer, I first need to determine which country this islet belongs to.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'In which country is the islet of Faiava Lasi located?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Faiava Lasi is an islet of Nukufetau, Tuvalu, which is immediately to the south of Lafaga islet at the North East of Nukufetau atoll.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the country where Faiava Lasi is located, we examine the context provided. The context states that Faiava Lasi is an islet of Nukufetau, Tuvalu. Since Nukufetau is part of Tuvalu, this directly identifies Tuvalu as the country where Faiava Lasi is located. Therefore, the answer is Tuvalu.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Tuvalu', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The main question asks about the discoverer of the country that contains the islet of Faiava Lasi. To answer this, I first need to identify which country contains Faiava Lasi, and since Faiava Lasi is a small islet in Tuvalu, the next step is to determine who discovered Tuvalu.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Who was the discoverer of Tuvalu?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', "In 1568, Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña was the first European to sail through the archipelago, sighting the island of Nui during his expedition in search of Terra Australis. In 1819 the island of Funafuti was named Ellice's Island; the name Ellice was applied to all nine islands after the work of English hydrographer Alexander George Findlay. The islands came under Britain's sphere of influence in the late 19th century, when each of the Ellice Islands was declared a British Protectorate by Captain Gibson of HMS Curacoa between 9 and 16 October 1892. The Ellice Islands were administered as British protectorate by a Resident Commissioner from 1892 to 1916 as part of the British Western Pacific Territories (BWPT), and then as part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands colony from 1916 to 1974.", '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The answer is formed by identifying the first European to sail through the Tuvalu archipelago, as mentioned in the context. The text states that in 1568, Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña was the first European to sail through the area, making him the discoverer of Tuvalu.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Álvaro de Mendaña', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the discoverer of the country containing the islet of Faiava Lasi, first identify the country where the islet is located, which is Tuvalu. Next, find the discoverer of Tuvalu, which is Álvaro de Mendaña. Therefore, the final answer is Álvaro de Mendaña.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Álvaro de Mendaña', '</answer>\n']}
{'id': 6513, 'user': 'Who won the mayoral election in the city where Daniel William Coquillett died?', 'assistant': ['<step>\n', 'The original question asks for the winner of the mayoral election in the city where Daniel William Coquillett died. To determine this, I first need to identify the specific city where Coquillett died, as that is the key location required to answer the question.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'In what city did Daniel William Coquillett die?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'Daniel William Coquillett (23 January 1856, Pleasant Valley, Ill. – 7 July 1911 Atlantic City, New Jersey) was an American entomologist who specialised in Diptera. He wrote a revision of the dipterous family Therevidae and many other scientific papers in which he described many new species and genera of Diptera. Coquillett was also the first to attempt fumigation with hydrocyanic acid as a means for controlling citrus scale insects. He experimented in the Wolfskill orange groves where he was supported by the foreman and later quarantine entomologist Alexander Craw in 1888-89.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the city where Daniel William Coquillett died, we examine the context provided. The text states that he was born in Pleasant Valley, Ill., and died in Atlantic City, New Jersey. The answer is directly given as "Atlantic City.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Atlantic City', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'The main question asks about the winner of the mayoral election in the city where Daniel William Coquillett died. To answer this, I first need to determine which city Coquillett died in, and the current sub-question focuses on Atlantic City, providing the necessary location context to proceed.', '</step>\n', '<subquery>\n', 'Who won the mayoral election in Atlantic City?', '</subquery>\n', '<retrieval>\n', 'The 2017 Atlantic City mayoral election was held on November 7, 2017 to elect the Mayor of Atlantic City, New Jersey. Primary elections were held on June 6. Incumbent Republican Don Guardian lost re-election to a second term to Democratic city councilman Frank Gilliam.', '</retrieval>\n', '<step>\n', 'The context states that in the 2017 Atlantic City mayoral election, incumbent Republican Don Guardian lost re-election to Democratic city councilman Frank Gilliam. Therefore, Frank Gilliam is the winner of the mayoral election.', '</step>\n', '<subanswer>\n', 'Frank Gilliam', '</subanswer>\n', '<step>\n', 'To determine the winner of the mayoral election in the city where Daniel William Coquillett died, first identify the city of his death, which is Atlantic City. Then, find out who won the mayoral election in Atlantic City. The answer is Frank Gilliam.', '</step>\n', '<answer>\n', 'Frank Gilliam', '</answer>\n']}
